// Min-heap: root is always the smallest; great for priority queues
PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
minHeap.offer(5); minHeap.offer(1); minHeap.offer(3);
System.out.println(minHeap.peek());   // 1 (min) in O(1)
// minHeap.contains(3) is O(n) - heaps don't support fast search
```

```java
// TreeSet is a balanced BST (red-black): fast ordered search + ranges
TreeSet<Integer> bst = new TreeSet<>();
bst.add(5); bst.add(1); bst.add(3);
System.out.println(bst.contains(3));  // true, O(log n)
System.out.println(bst.ceiling(2));   // 3 - range query
System.out.println(bst.first());      // 1 - but min is O(log n), not O(1)
```
Heap wins for repeated min/max; BST wins for searching and range queries.
